---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ShapeIcon from '../components/ShapeIcon';
import { faceShapePath, shapes, titleCase } from '../config';
import { shapeGuide } from '../content';
---
<Base title="Face Shape Guide: Understand the Six Common Outlines" description="Learn what oval, round, square, heart, diamond and oblong face shapes mean, how they overlap, and how we compare facial proportions." breadcrumb="Face shape guide">
<div class="page-head"><span class="eyebrow">YOUR OUTLINE, EXPLAINED</span><h1>A guide to face shapes.</h1><p>Six useful descriptions. Countless individual variations. Get familiar with the shapes before you explore your own.</p></div>
<div class="container"><div class="guide-grid">{shapes.map(shape=><article class="guide-card" id={shape}><ShapeIcon shape={shape}/><h2>{titleCase(shape)}</h2><p>{shapeGuide[shape].detail}</p><p class="tip">{shapeGuide[shape].tip}</p><a class="text-link" href={faceShapePath(shape)}>{titleCase(shape)} face shape guide ↗</a></article>)}</div></div>
<article class="article-layout"><h2>How we describe a shape</h2><p>The tool compares visible face length, cheekbone width, estimated upper-face width and jaw width. A lower-face contour angle adds a small amount of information about angularity. Each measurement is normalized, so the result does not depend on the size of the displayed photograph.</p><p>MediaPipe supplies landmarks, not a face shape label. Our own rule-based engine compares your measurements with six documented geometric prototypes. A score of 80 means a closer match to a prototype than 50; it does not mean an 80% probability of having that shape.</p><h2>Between two categories? That is normal.</h2><p>Categories overlap. A long face with a broad jaw may resemble both oblong and square outlines. The results include the two leading matches, all six scores, and an explanation of the measurements. A small score gap is labeled as a mixed result.</p><h2>What a photo cannot tell us</h2><p>A single photograph cannot reveal your full three-dimensional structure. Hairline position is not reliably visible, so upper-face width is always an estimate. Focal length, head pose, facial hair, expression and lighting can change the apparent outline. Use these descriptions as a starting point for exploration.</p><a class="button primary" href="/face-shape-detector/">Explore my face shape ↗</a></article>
</Base>
